    "local": {
      "heading": "What makes a Pennsylvania replacement different",
      "paras": [
        "Pennsylvania splits between fuels: 46% of homes heat water with gas, 45% with electricity and 6% with fuel oil. Gas is the cheaper tank to run here, about $247 a year against $656 for a standard electric tank, so a heat pump mainly pays off in homes that already have electric water heat.",
        "Pennsylvania's utility rebates come from its Act 129 efficiency programs, which run in multi-year phases; PPL's page notes a program change after May 2026, so check the current phase before you count on an amount. Incoming water drops to about 45.4 °F in February."
      ]
    },
    "highlights": [
      "Pennsylvania is split between fuels: 46% of homes heat water with gas and 45% with electricity, so check which you have before you compare quotes; a fuel switch adds a gas line and vent, or a 240-volt circuit.",
      "6% of Pennsylvania homes still heat water with fuel oil, often from the heating boiler; those systems are priced differently from the tanks on this page.",
      "Where you live inside Pennsylvania matters: the same 50-gallon gas job is about $1,833 in the Philadelphia-Camden-Wilmington, PA-NJ-DE-MD area and $1,750 in the Scranton--Wilkes-Barre, PA area.",
      "Philadelphia requires a master plumber and a signed certification."
    ]
